INSERT INTO page_sections (page_id, sort_order, section_type, variant, content, entity_refs, asset_refs, editable) SELECT p.id, 3, 'page_content', 'live', '{"h1":"East Java Destinations: Volcanoes, Waterfalls & Coastline","faq":[{"a":"For many travelers, Bromo is the easiest first East Java anchor because it offers strong volcanic scenery without the same screening and sulfur exposure logic as Ijen.","q":"What is the easiest destination to start with?"},{"a":"No. Ijen needs more respect than a typical scenic stop because health screening, sulfur conditions, and night-oriented route timing matter.","q":"Is Ijen just another volcano stop?"},{"a":"Usually both together. A destination only makes sense when you understand how it behaves inside the wider route structure, timing, and physical demands.","q":"Should I choose by destination or by route?"}],"lede":["East Java rewards travelers who prepare well. This hub introduces the main places guests ask about most often and explains them in a way that supports better planning, not just better browsing."],"sections":[{"copy":"Bromo and Ijen are the two volcanic anchors of most JVTO itineraries, but they are not interchangeable. Each destination carries different access logic, timing, terrain, and safety considerations.","items":[{"copy":"More accessible than Ijen for many travelers, but still shaped by cold air, early starts, exposed terrain, and changing visibility.","title":"Mount Bromo"},{"copy":"One of East Java''s most distinctive destinations, but also one of the easiest to misunderstand if health screening, sulfur conditions, and route timing are treated lightly.","title":"Ijen Crater"}],"title":"Volcanoes"},{"copy":"Madakaripura and Tumpak Sewu are visually dramatic but operationally different. Water exposure, footing, stairs, and weather conditions all affect how these places should be approached.","items":[{"copy":"A strong contrast to Bromo-focused routes, with canyon-like water exposure and a more immersive waterfall atmosphere.","title":"Madakaripura Waterfall"},{"copy":"A more serious waterfall segment that should be understood as real terrain, not a soft scenic add-on.","title":"Tumpak Sewu Waterfall"}],"title":"Waterfalls"},{"copy":"Papuma adds a different rhythm to the East Java route system. It is useful for guests who want a broader landscape mix instead of a volcano-only itinerary.","items":[{"copy":"Best understood as a pacing and contrast decision inside longer East Java journeys, not just an isolated scenic stop.","title":"Papuma Beach"}],"title":"Coastal Highlights"},{"copy":"Use destination pages to understand the place itself, then move into the relevant tours to understand how it fits into an actual route, transfer pattern, and travel schedule.","items":[{"copy":"Best for flexibility, especially if you want short volcano routes or a broader menu of overland combinations.","title":"Surabaya Departures"},{"copy":"Best for guided cross-island continuity when you want East Java included without self-managing the harder logistics.","title":"Bali Departures"},{"copy":"Destinations should be matched with your route seriousness, not just your visual wishlist.","title":"Preparation Matters"}],"title":"Which Tours Include These Places"}],"destinationLinks":[{"href":"/destinations/mount-bromo","label":"Mount Bromo","description":"Open the Bromo destination page for sunrise timing, route logic, and practical expectations."},{"href":"/destinations/ijen-crater","label":"Ijen Crater","description":"Open the Ijen destination page for blue fire reality, health screening context, and trek seriousness."},{"href":"/destinations/madakaripura-waterfall","label":"Madakaripura Waterfall","description":"Open the Madakaripura page for water exposure, footing, and route-fit context."},{"href":"/destinations/tumpak-sewu-waterfall","label":"Tumpak Sewu Waterfall","description":"Open the Tumpak Sewu page for trek reality, access seriousness, and route-readiness context."},{"href":"/destinations/papuma-beach","label":"Papuma Beach","description":"Open the Papuma page for coastline pacing logic and longer East Java route context."}],"body_md":"# /destinations — Hub Page Copy\n\n---\n\n## Hero\n\n**Five destinations. One licensed operator for all of them.**\n\nEvery site on JVTO''s East Java circuit — volcano craters, canyon waterfalls, a coastal headland — is reached by dedicated private vehicle, with BBKSDA park clearance already in hand.\n\n---\n\n## Destination Cards\n\n### Kawah Ijen — Ijen Crater\n\n**Elevation**: 2,386 m\n\nThe world''s highest-volume acidic crater lake sits inside an active stratovolcano in Banyuwangi. Pre-dawn, sulfuric gases ignite at the crater floor — a blue-fire phenomenon visible in suitable weather conditions. You share the trail with sulfur miners carrying 70–90 kg loads. There is no other experience like it in Java.\n\n**Slug**: `/destinations/ijen-crater`\n\n---\n\n### Mount Bromo\n\n**Elevation**: 2,329 m\n\nBromo sits inside the vast Tengger caldera — a volcanic sea of sand framed by sheer caldera walls. The Penanjakan sunrise viewpoint at 2,770 m is one of Southeast Asia''s most photographed scenes. Access is by 4WD jeep across the caldera floor at 03:00. The BBKSDA national-park clearance is already arranged.\n\n**Slug**: `/destinations/mount-bromo`\n\n---\n\n### Tumpak Sewu Waterfall\n\n**Height**: ~120 m (multi-tiered curtain)\n\nA curved cliff face in Lumajang Regency sends dozens of streams over its edge in a continuous curtain drop. Known as Java''s Niagara. Most visitors stand at the rim. Those who descend the ~300 steps into the canyon stand at the base, surrounded by mist. JVTO guides assess fitness before the descent begins — the choice to go down stays with the guest.\n\n**Slug**: `/destinations/tumpak-sewu-waterfall`\n\n---\n\n### Madakaripura Waterfall\n\n**Distinction**: Tallest waterfall in Java — main curtain drop ~100 m\n\nA narrow canyon in Probolinggo Regency walls you in for ~1.5 km before the gorge opens into a horseshoe of falling water. Bring a raincoat — the spray reaches everywhere. The site is said to be where Gajah Mada, the 14th-century Majapahit prime minister, spent his final days.\n\n**Slug**: `/destinations/madakaripura-waterfall`\n\n---\n\n### Papuma Beach\n\n**Feature**: White-sand beach + cape headland (~86 m), Jember Regency\n\nThe only coastal stop on a JVTO East Java circuit. After Ijen''s 2,386 m night hike, Papuma resets the pace — white sand, offshore rock formations, and a short headland walk rising to ~86 m with views along the coast. Positioned between the volcano hike legs and the canyon waterfall stops on Papuma-family packages.\n\n**Slug**: `/destinations/papuma-beach`\n\n---\n\n## Transport Note\n\nEvery destination on this list is reached by dedicated private vehicle — AC MPV for smaller groups, Toyota Hiace for larger ones. No public buses. No shared transfers. Your vehicle, your driver, your schedule."}'::jsonb, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], false FROM pages p WHERE p.route = '/destinations' ON CONFLICT (page_id, sort_order) DO UPDATE SET section_type = EXCLUDED.section_type, variant = EXCLUDED.variant, content = EXCLUDED.content, entity_refs = EXCLUDED.entity_refs, asset_refs = EXCLUDED.asset_refs WHERE page_sections.editable IS NOT TRUE;
